    {
      "sectionId": "section-dexterous-feet",
      "heading": "Dexterous Feet",
      "sourceAnchor": "dexterous-feet",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "I can’t reproduce the exact text of the rules because the Hadozee isn’t included in the SRD. Grab your copy of Spelljammer: Adventures in Space, or check the "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Hadozee",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/races/1121699-thri-kreen#HadozeeTraits"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " page on DnDBeyond. The precise text is important, so I encourage you to read the text of Dexterous Feet before continuing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "To paraphrase what your feet "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "can"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " do:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-3",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Manipulate objects"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Open/close doors/containers"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Pick up or set down Tiny objects"
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are several things which a character can typically do with their hands which are notably omitted. This isn’t a comprehensive list, but here are some noteworthy things that you "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "can’t"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " do with your feet:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-5",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Anything that requires “a free hand”, such as Grapple or Shove"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Grapple or Shove a Creature"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Reload a weapon with the “Ammunition” property"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Perform somatic components"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use a magic item"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use a shield"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use an Object (the Action)"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Wield a weapon"
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dexterous-feet-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There’s an important distinction between the phrase “manipulate an object” and the Action “Use an Object”. “Use an Object” includes things like using alchemist’s fire. Manipulating an object just means to handle it, such as by turning it over, carrying it, poking it, etc. A hadozee could flip a table, but they couldn’t drink a potion with their Secondary Arms. I believe that this was a deliberate balancing decision to prevent hadozee characters from breaking the “hand economy” too much."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-glide",
      "heading": "Glide",
      "sourceAnchor": "glide",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-glide-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Since you can’t always rely on climbing, maximizing glide requires understanding the mechanics for both jumping and falling, which gets much harder with the errata that WotC issued almost immediately following Spelljammer’s release (you now need to fall 10 feet and can only glide an amount equal to your land speed). First, you need to get 10 feet into the air and fall. A character can high jump 3+Str with a running start, so aside from barbarians who have hit 24 Strength at level 20, your character will require magical assistance to reliably activate glide. Look for the Jump spell or magic items like Boots of Striding and Springing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-glide-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Provided that you can trigger Glide, doing so takes no action on your part and does not consume any of your own movement beyond potentially the 20 feet spent to trigger it (10 feet running start, 10 feet jump). Provided that your speed is 30 feet, and that you take a running start, jump 10 feet up and forward, and then walk 10 feet following your Glide, you can move double your walking speed every time that you move at the cost of your Reaction."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Classes",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you have 12 or more Strength, casting Jump makes Glide usable, and at level 10 you can infuse Boots of Striding and Springing so that you’re not spending a spell slot every time you use it, making Glide an effective way to get around. Infusing Winged Boots is arguably a better choice, but it’s certainly not as fun."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Hadozee Dodge provides excellent protection when combined with Rage’s damage resistance. Dexterous Feet might be useful if you fight with an item in each hand. Glide won’t be useful for most barbarians until level 20."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Path of the Beast gets the ability to leap incredible distances, allowing you to leap high into the air and trigger Glide."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’re basically only here for Hadozee Dodge and Dexterous Feet. Unless you can find a way to make Dexterous Feet matter, just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’re basically only here for Hadozee Dodge and Dexterous Feet. Unless you can find a way to make Dexterous Feet matter, just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’re basically only here for Hadozee Dodge and Dexterous Feet. Unless you can find a way to make Dexterous Feet matter, just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You could use Wild Shape combined with Glide to fly high into the air before reverting to your natural form to use Glide, but why go to the trouble? Wild Shape lasts for an hour. Turn into a fast bird if you need to travel long distances."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’re basically only here for Hadozee Dodge and Dexterous Feet. Unless you can find a way to make Dexterous Feet matter, just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Eldritch Knight can cast Jump, but you don’t have enough spell slots to justify doing so in order to trigger Glide."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Even if you can somehow trigger it, Glide is less useful for the Monk since monks already get Slow Fall. Hadozee Dodge is nice, but at that point play a goliath. You can get closer to trigger Glide by using Step of the Wind (it doubles jump distance), but you still need much more Strength than the Monk can afford in order to do so."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You’re basically only here for Hadozee Dodge and Dexterous Feet. Unless you can find a way to make Dexterous Feet matter, just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterous Feet is occasionally useful for rangers relying on two-weapon fighting. You’ll get some use out of Hadozee Dodge, but at that point just play a goliath."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rogues don’t have the Strength to make jumping and gliding useful, and if you want to use items as a Bonus Action the Thief makes Dexterous Feet entirely obsolete with "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Fast Hands",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/rogue/fast-hands/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Hadozee Dodge is still good, but Uncanny Dodge is both more effective and not restricted by a daily usage limitation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Thief’s Second-Story Work feature adds your Dexterity modifier to the distance covered when you make a running jump. With 14 Strength and 20 Dexterity, you can hit the 10 feet required to trigger Glide.  "
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you have 12 Strength and cast Jump, Glide provides an inexpensive substitute for flight and teleportation in some situations. Hadozee Dodge is an excellent pad to the Sorcerer’s d6 hit dice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Storm Sorcery also allows you to move yourself 10 feet into the air, allowing you to Glide without relying on Jump or a magic item."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Warlocks have no use for Strength and can’t cast Jump, unless you use the Otherworldly Leap invocation. Even then, you need 12 Strength to get high enough to trigger Glide. That mildly annoying investment is the easiest and most accessible way to reliably use Glide, making the Warlock a go-to option for the Hadozee. Other classes should strongly consider a class dip or the Eldritch Adept feat if they can’t guarantee access to Boots of Striding and Springing or a similar item."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Ascendant Step invocation allows you to cast Levitate on yourself at will, allowing to move yourself arbitrarily high into the air. It’s not entirely clear what happens when you end the spell and attempt to Glide, but the vertical movement is so slow that this isn’t really useful so much as it is mildly entertaining."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Otherwordly Leap makes Jump free, but you still need 12 Strength to jump high enough to trigger Glide."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If you have 12 Strength and cast Jump, Glide provides an inexpensive substitute for flight and teleportation in some situations. Hadozee Dodge is an excellent pad to the Wizard’s d6 hit dice."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
